## Demo Player Synchronisation Pattern

Every step has two phases. NEVER use `waitForTimeout`; always synchronise on
button state:

```
READING phase  →  [aria-label="Next step"] is ENABLED  (except last step — see below)
ACTION phase   →  [aria-label="Next step"] is DISABLED
```

**Last step exception:** On step N/N, Next is **always** disabled. Use `finishDemoStep()`
instead of waiting for Next after entering the final step. Full details:
[`e2e/DEMO-LESSON-E2E-MEMO.md`](../e2e/DEMO-LESSON-E2E-MEMO.md).

```typescript
// ✅ Correct
await waitForReadingPhase(page);  // wait for reading
await page.locator('[aria-label="Next step"]').click();  // start action
await waitForReadingPhase(page);  // wait for action to finish

// ❌ Wrong — fragile, fails on slow machines
await page.waitForTimeout(1500);
await nextBtn.click();
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
```

---

## Do NOT Use Playwright MCP for Demo Validation

The Playwright MCP (`browser_evaluate`, `browser_click`, `browser_take_screenshot`)
is useful for **one-off exploration** — finding a selector, diagnosing a crash,
quick smoke-check on a live page.

It is **not acceptable** for demo validation because:
- Each MCP call is a round-trip (slow)
- No `waitFor` semantics — only `sleep` hacks
- Zero reusability across sessions
- No assertion framework — only console.log checks

After you use MCP to explore and discover something, encode the finding in a
Playwright spec. Next time, run the spec instead of re-exploring.

---

## Screenshots

All screenshots go to `e2e/screenshots/` (gitignored). Use `takeNamedScreenshot()`
so names are consistent and timestamped. Never save to `/tmp/` or the project root.
